      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "1f4b0ce4-44e5-5302-a0f6-1f82393264ef",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Karlheinz Pintsch, Rudolf Hess, Adolf Hitler, Fredric Warburg, William Shirer, Lobsang Rampa, Pierre Boulle\n- Roles: Deputy Führer, Hitler's deputy\n- Places: Scotland, United Kingdom\n- Products: Mein Kampf, The Third Eye, The Bridge over the River Kwai, The Rise and Fall of the Third Reich\n- Events: Rudolf Hess's May 1941 flight to Scotland, World War II, Hess's imprisonment, Hess's conviction, Hess's suicide\n- Topics: historiographical debate\n\nFacts:\n- In 1933, Rudolf Hess was appointed Deputy Führer to Adolf Hitler.\n- Rudolf Hess edited Adolf Hitler's book Mein Kampf.\n- In May 1941, Rudolf Hess flew solo to Scotland in an attempt to negotiate peace with the United Kingdom during World War II.\n- Karlheinz Pintsch had to report Rudolf Hess's illegal May 1941 flight to Scotland to Adolf Hitler.\n- Karlheinz Pintsch's recollections and notes about Rudolf Hess's May 1941 flight have been debated by historians.\n- After flying to Scotland, Rudolf Hess was taken prisoner.\n- Rudolf Hess was eventually convicted of crimes against peace and served a life sentence until his suicide.\n- Fredric Warburg's notable publications included Adolf Hitler's Mein Kampf, Lobsang Rampa's The Third Eye, Pierre Boulle's The Bridge over the River Kwai, and William Shirer's The Rise and Fall of the Third Reich.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "6980b596-b98b-5366-b491-53ff981aaf24",
        "text": "Page: Karlheinz Pintsch. Sentence: It fell to him to report Hess's illegal May 1941 flight to Scotland to Hitler and his recollections and notes have been the subject of debate by historians.\nPage: Rudolf Hess. Sentence: Appointed Deputy Führer to Adolf Hitler in 1933, he served in this position until 1941, when he flew solo to Scotland in an attempt to negotiate peace with the United Kingdom during World War II.\nPage: Rudolf Hess. Sentence: He was taken prisoner and eventually was convicted of crimes against peace, serving a life sentence until his suicide.\nPage: Mein Kampf. Sentence: The book was edited by Hitler's deputy Rudolf Hess.\nPage: Fredric Warburg. Sentence: Other notable publications included \"The Third Eye\" by Lobsang Rampa, Pierre Boulle's \"The Bridge over the River Kwai\", Adolf Hitler's \"Mein Kampf\" and William Shirer's \"The Rise and Fall of the Third Reich\".",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
